---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="How Image Processing Works | Browser vs Server"
  description="Diagram and rules for which compressimage.fun tools upload, how jobs are tokenized, when files delete, and which utilities never leave the browser."
  canonicalPath="/how-processing-works"
  pageType="article"
  h1="How processing works"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'How processing works', path: '/how-processing-works' },
  ]}
>
  <header class="hero compact">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">Architecture you can quote</div>
      <h1>How an image moves through compressimage.fun</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two paths exist. Mixing them in copy is how sites accidentally claim “no upload” for a
        server compressor.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <article class="article shell">
    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="article-summary">
        Compression, conversion, resize, crop, rotate, metadata, watermarks, favicons, and form-photo
        tools upload to the same origin. Base64, Image to PDF, and the color picker do not.
      </p>
      <section>
        <h2>Server path</h2>
        <ol class="flow-list">
          <li>The browser sends the file to <code>/api</code> on compressimage.fun.</li>
          <li>Nginx proxies to the private Fastify processor. The job ID is random.</li>
          <li>A separate high-entropy bearer token authorizes preview, download, and delete.</li>
          <li>Sharp/libvips decodes, transforms, and writes outputs on an isolated volume.</li>
          <li>You download. Delete now wipes the directory. Otherwise TTL cleanup runs within four hours.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre class="flow-diagram" aria-label="Server processing flow">{`browser file
    → HTTPS same-origin POST /api
    → nginx (noindex, no-store)
    → processor job directory (random names)
    → Sharp/libvips
    → preview / download with bearer token
    → Delete now or TTL ≤ 4 hours`}</pre>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Browser path</h2>
        <pre class="flow-diagram" aria-label="Browser processing flow">{`file or pasted text
    → this tab’s memory
    → canvas / encoder / text output
    → download from the browser
    → no /api upload of the file`}</pre>
        <p>
          Image to PDF, color picker, and the Base64 suite follow this path. They still fetch CSS,
          JS, and (if configured) analytics scripts for the page itself.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Exact-size search</h2>
        <p>
          A codec quality number is not a kilobyte target. The optimizer keeps original dimensions,
          tries a bounded quality search, measures encoded bytes, and only then walks a fixed scale
          sequence. It never pads a file up to the cap. If nothing fits, it says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          See the <a href="/compression-tests">published fixture measurements</a> and the
          <a href="/guides/how-to-compress-image-to-exact-file-size">exact-size guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Limits that are real, not marketing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Default per-file upload ceiling: 100 MB.</li>
          <li>Default batch: 50 files and 500 MB combined.</li>
          <li>Default pixel ceiling: 100 megapixels.</li>
          <li>Animated sources are detected and rejected in this version.</li>
          <li>Unsafe SVG (scripts, event handlers, foreign objects, remote references) is rejected.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
